# Fix C++ interface code from dcomp.idl and dwrite*.idl.
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12720 state=closed
# This MR tackles several issues for C++ generated code when handling
dcomp.idl and dwrite\_*.idl (mostly dwrite_3.idl).

Note: native SDK doesn't use .idl files for these interfaces, but
interface definition directly embedded in C/C++ through dedicated
extension.
AFAICT, gcc/g++ doesn't support it.

The issues mainly cope with some interface requires overloaded
method declarations that standard IDL doesn't support.

I opted for extending widl to support these, and left other
solutions aside:
- adding interface (in the MS way) support to gcc/g++/mingw,
- replacing the Wine .idl files with properly generated .h
  files; either done by hand (I don't feel like it), or
  get the help of a tool to do it (adapting widl comes to
  mind).

List of issues addressed by this MR:
1/ as regular .idl doesn't support overloaded methods in interfaces,
   and as C wouldn't support the unique name of overloaded methods
   in various places (struct fields, macros definitions, functions...),
   current Wine .idl is written with adding suffixes to overloaded methods
   to generate unique names
   => the generated C++ interface (struct with pure virtual) uses the
      suffixed methods names, breaking callers using the expected overloaded
      method name.

2/ if interface A inherits from interface B, and both A and B define a method
   of same name 'f', yet with different signature, default C++ inheritance
   rules don't let B::f be visible directly from A. It has to be explicitely
   introduced into A scope.
   [native SDK has explicit 'using B::f;' in the interface definition;
    Wine .idl doesn't]
   => the B::f method is not callable from A (compilation error)

3/ vtable layout. MS compiler doesn't always layout the vtable entries
   in the order where the virtual methods have been declared in C++ struct/class.
   Basically, all overloaded methods of name XX are grouped together at the
   declaration index of the first one, yet with reversing their order of declaration.
